readers.summary:
In this partition the conjugate-space-monopole idea first appears, and in the notebook only, as row 8 of #1's predictions table (v0.1, September 2026): 'Permanent null (p-polarized solitons); Berry-curvature monopoles in solids as the only shadow', killed by 'Direct monopole detection'. P4 supplies the separation: 'Gauge interactions do not connect the two representations.' Addenda v0.2-v0.6 add nothing on monopoles; v0.2 and calc note #4 only carry the permanent nulls forward. #9 keeps row 8 without '(p-polarized solitons)' and has no charge-lattice section, knot candidate or Appendix A. #11 §11, the v2.0 knot trilemma ('topological knots, the conjugate sector's monopoles'), Appendix A items 5 and 11, and #14 all predate the 2026-10-19 import, so git cannot date them. The initial README (2026-10-18) calls the repo a hypothesis 'explaining dark matter and missing magnetic monopoles' and is unchanged (flag 37, unruled).

On 2026-10-19 git shows the identification of the magnetic sector with the conjugate sector weakening step by step. #14 v2 conjectured the p-polarized hidden sector to be 'the S-dual image of the electric sector', with α_d ≈ 34 fixed by S. v3 kept only 'identified with the momentum-polarized half', used the charge lattice 'only as motivation', assumed no exact electric-magnetic duality, and said the objects 'carry no charge under our photon'. Round 3 failed v3 on the Kibble normalization (p ≈ 104, not 0.023). #14 was withdrawn, P4 made strict, and #11's trilemma became an obituary.

Standing now:
- the explanation is dead;
- conjugate-sector monopoles as ontology are alive but have no candidate;
- the clumping loophole is untested;
- one modular structure for both polarizations is an unqueued [O] conjecture.
The later notes are silent on monopoles; 'soliton' in #3 and #4 means a wave-dark-matter core.
